{
  "items": [
    {"n": 1, "name": "First screen", "score": 2, "reason": "The headline says what to do: turn on a retry whichever planner is chosen. It also says what is unresolved: nobody declared what a point of pass rate is worth. The lede gives the price band where each model is the best buy, and the decision block shows the trade. Nano-XS at high reasoning with a retry costs $444 a month; Brisk-S at low reasoning with a retry costs $1,208 and passes 2.4 points more. The block flags the main risk: the step above the lean, +0.8 (-0.4 to +2.1), can't be separated from zero, and the price per point decides. I recomputed $319.67, $1,595.18 and $17,236.36 from price_tradeoff, and they match."},
    {"n": 2, "name": "Coherent story", "score": 2, "reason": "Each claim matches its values table. In frontier, every Atlas-M configuration except the high-reasoning one with a retry is beaten by a cheaper Brisk-S one (for example 97.9% at $1.655 against 98.0% at $0.633). In retry, +1.9 (+0.3 to +3.4) is set against +0.8 (-0.4 to +2.1). In retry_models, the gap between models is +5.1 (+1.8 to +8.5). In multihop, Brisk-S gains 1.7 points on multi-hop against 0.1 on single lookups. In latency, the slowest Brisk-S p95 is 870 ms and the fastest Atlas-M p95 is 900 ms. The lede says a retry's gain is clear of zero in eight of nine pairings. I checked the closest case, Atlas-M at low reasoning: +1.4 (+0.1 to +2.7), so it holds."},
    {"n": 3, "name": "Honest emphasis", "score": 2, "reason": "The lean is marked as our own call, low-confidence and conditional on the price band. The step above it is shown with its interval and its 9.4% chance of being read the wrong way round. The page names the lean's longer latency tail (465 ms to 513 ms at p95) and its likely best-of-eighteen inflation. One small overreach is in retry's caption: it says reasoning's gain 'shrinks' once a retry is on, but none of the three interaction intervals clears zero (see finding)."},
    {"n": 4, "name": "Sound elision", "score": 2, "reason": "Nothing omitted would change the decision. The calls-per-task omission now names the band edges it would move, and the never-best-buy omission now names Brisk-S at low reasoning with no retry as the exception that matters under a floor. options_dominance makes no call because cost is the only declared axis, which is consistent with the page's trade framing."},
    {"n": 5, "name": "Confounds handled", "score": 2, "reason": "Each confound is carried with what would make it matter, and each has a next step: no control (paired rerun), best of eighteen (fresh run), unknown routing and unnamed volume unit (record the provider and what the volume counts). The confidence's High now covers only the retry finding and the frontier's shape."},
    {"n": 6, "name": "Actionable next steps", "score": 2, "reason": "Each step names what it settles. A declared price, floor or latency budget turns the trade into a decision. The paired rerun at about 5,432 cases per arm confirms or refutes high reasoning's gain on Brisk-S. A run of the multi-hop reasoning mix prices it. Recording provider and volume unit fixes who the costs belong to and where the band edges sit."},
    {"n": 7, "name": "Evidence fits claims", "score": 2, "reason": "Each claim's own figure is in its block. retry now has a cost table, retry_models now holds the Atlas-M high-reasoning row (+0.9, -0.2 to +2), and atlas shows the gap and both costs. Two small fit issues: retry's cost table rounds $0.288 and $0.302 to $0.29 and $0.30, which blurs the $0.014 gap the sentence leans on, and failures says the Nano-XS card is the kind a retry catches although it came from a configuration that already had a retry (see findings)."}
  ],
  "findings": [
    {"claim": "retry", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "Three fixes in this claim's blocks. (1) The diverging-bars caption says the last rows show reasoning's gain shrinking once a retry is on. All three interaction intervals include zero: -1.7 (-4.2 to +0.9), -2.2 (-4.6 to +0.2) and -0.5 (-2.5 to +1.5). Say the point estimates shrink but the run cannot tell this from zero. (2) The interval +0.03535 to +2.6 prints at a different precision from every other row; print it as +0.04 to +2.6. (3) The cost table rounds Brisk-S at low reasoning to $0.29 and $0.30, while the sentence cites $0.288 to $0.302. Print three decimals so the block shows the $0.014 gap the claim rests on.", "continues": "new"},
    {"claim": "failures", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The sentence calls the fourth card 'malformed output of the kind a retry catches'. That card (TQ-0051) comes from Nano-XS at low reasoning with a retry already on, so the retry did not catch this one. Reword to something like 'malformed output that survived a retry; the card's note says a retry recovers 70% of these'. The sentence should also say these are the few kept cards, not a measured breakdown of failures, as the caption already does.", "continues": "new"}
  ],
  "resolved": ["f1.1", "f1.2", "f1.3", "f1.4", "f1.5", "f1.6"]
}
